    "perplexity": {
      "description": "...Famous Last Words... is the seventh studio album by British rock band Supertramp, released in October 1982 on A&M Records (later under Universal), marking a pivotal moment as frontman Roger Hodgson's final effort with the group before his departure. Clocking in at 47-48 minutes across nine tracks, it blends the band's signature progressive pop-rock style with melodic hooks, piano-driven arrangements, and introspective lyrics, exemplified by hits like \"It's Raining Again\" and \"Crazy.\" The album captures the creative tensions between Hodgson and Rick Davies, whose songwriting rivalry shaped Supertramp's sound, though it leaned toward Hodgson's whimsical, atmospheric contributions amid the band's evolving commercial phase post-Breakfast in America.\n\nMusically, it features upbeat anthems such as \"Put On Your Old Brown Shoes\" and \"Bonnie,\" alongside more reflective pieces, maintaining Supertramp's fusion of art rock complexity and radio-friendly accessibility. The iconic cover art—a tightrope walker mid-fall with scissors severing the rope—symbolically foreshadowed the band's impending breakup, a detail that intrigued young fans and underscored themes of precarious balance. Reception was mixed; while it achieved moderate chart success, critics noted it as less innovative than earlier classics like Crime of the Century, yet it remains a nostalgic staple for '80s rock enthusiasts, with remastered editions (e.g., 2021) preserving its polished production.\n\nIts significance lies in closing Supertramp's classic era, bridging their prog-rock roots with pop sensibilities, and offering enduring tracks that highlight Hodgson's melodic gifts before his solo pivot. Though not their peak, the album's emotional resonance grew retrospectively, especially following Davies' passing, cementing its place in the band's discography as a bittersweet farewell.",
